      </svg>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <aside class="space-y-4 lg:mt-2" aria-label="Background concepts">
        <details class="panel group overflow-hidden">
          <summary
            class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 px-6 py-5 [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden"
          >
            <h3 class="font-serif text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">What is retrieval?</h3>
            <span
              aria-hidden
              class="grid h-7 w-7 shrink-0 place-items-center rounded-full border border-ink-200 text-ink-600 transition-transform group-open:rotate-45"
              >+</span
            >
          </summary>
          <div class="border-t border-ink-100 px-6 py-5">
            <p class="text-[0.95rem] italic leading-relaxed text-ink-600">
              Retrieval is the part of the system that finds and gathers the information an AI
              agent needs to do its job. Instead of expecting the agent to “just know” everything,
              retrieval pulls the most relevant documents, records, or data from the
              organization’s sources and gives them to the agent at the right time.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details class="panel group overflow-hidden">
          <summary
            class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 px-6 py-5 [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden"
          >
            <h3 class="font-serif text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">
              Orchestration layer vs. context engine
            </h3>
            <span
              aria-hidden
              class="grid h-7 w-7 shrink-0 place-items-center rounded-full border border-ink-200 text-ink-600 transition-transform group-open:rotate-45"
              >+</span
            >
          </summary>
          <div class="space-y-3 border-t border-ink-100 px-6 py-5">
            <p class="text-[0.95rem] italic leading-relaxed text-ink-600">
              A context engine helps deliver useful information to an agent. It focuses on giving
              the agent the right context from the organization’s data.
            </p>
            <p class="text-[0.95rem] italic leading-relaxed text-ink-600">
              An orchestration layer governs how the overall system behaves. It decides what
              happens first, which agent acts next, when the system should pause or escalate, and
              how the workflow moves from step to step.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </aside>
    </div>

    <Callout kind="rationale" title="The source-of-truth inversion">
      The PRD remains the human stakeholder anchor, but the source of truth for execution shifts
      into the spec stack. The real product of governance is not a markdown document. It is an
      evidence-aware runtime package that tells the system what it may retrieve, how it should
      behave, and when it must stop.
    </Callout>

    <DetailExpander summary="What executable context actually contains" kind="spec" client:visible>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5">
        {executableContext.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
      </ul>
    </DetailExpander>

    <div class="prose-narrative mt-16 max-w-3xl">
      <IntroBody.Content />
    </div>
  </NarrativeSection>

  <NarrativeSection id="approach" tone="default" width="wide">
    <div class="grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1.4fr)_minmax(18rem,0.8fr)]">
      <div>
        <SectionHero
          eyebrow={approach.data.eyebrow}
          title={approach.data.title}
          lede={approach.data.lede}
        />
        <div class="prose-narrative mt-8 max-w-3xl">
          <ApproachBody.Content />
        </div>
      </div>

      <aside class="space-y-4 lg:mt-2" aria-label="Background concepts">
        <details class="panel group overflow-hidden">
          <summary
            class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 px-6 py-5 [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden"
          >
            <h3 class="font-serif text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">What is chunking?</h3>
            <span
              aria-hidden
              class="grid h-7 w-7 shrink-0 place-items-center rounded-full border border-ink-200 text-ink-600 transition-transform group-open:rotate-45"
              >+</span
            >
          </summary>
          <div class="border-t border-ink-100 px-6 py-5">
            <p class="text-[0.95rem] italic leading-relaxed text-ink-600">
              Chunking is the process of breaking large documents into smaller, usable pieces so
              the system can work with them more effectively. Instead of handing an agent an
              entire policy or report at once, the system splits it into meaningful sections that
              are easier to store, search, and retrieve later.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details class="panel group overflow-hidden">
          <summary
            class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 px-6 py-5 [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden"
          >
            <h3 class="font-serif text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">What is embedding?</h3>
            <span
              aria-hidden
              class="grid h-7 w-7 shrink-0 place-items-center rounded-full border border-ink-200 text-ink-600 transition-transform group-open:rotate-45"
              >+</span
            >
          </summary>
          <div class="border-t border-ink-100 px-6 py-5">
            <p class="text-[0.95rem] italic leading-relaxed text-ink-600">
              Embedding is the process of turning a piece of text into a numerical representation
              that helps the system find related meaning, not just matching keywords. This makes
              it possible to search for information based on semantic similarity — for example,
              finding a relevant policy section even if it does not use the exact same wording as
              the question.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </aside>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-12">
      <p class="eyebrow mb-3 text-accent">The document stack</p>
      <SpecHierarchy />
    </div>

    <div class="mt-12 grid items-center gap-8 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,1.6fr)]">
      <StatementCallout>
        GitHub Spec Kit centers the spec. Kiro centers the workflow. <span class="text-accent"
          >This model centers the governed translation from enterprise intent into agent-usable
          context.</span
        >
      </StatementCallout>
      <SchoolsOfThought schools={schools} />
    </div>

    <Callout kind="architecture" title="Hybrid search is not the full story">
      Hybrid search is the engine. Agentic retrieval is the planner, router, judge, and assembler
      around it. The system does not win because it has vectors and keywords. It wins because those
      capabilities are put inside a governed runtime path.
    </Callout>

    <div class="mt-14 grid items-start gap-6 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1.4fr)_minmax(18rem,20rem)]">
      <div class="panel p-6">
        <p class="eyebrow mb-4">Retrieval flow</p>
        <ol class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
          {
            retrievalSteps.map((step, index) => (
              <li class="rounded-2xl border border-ink-100 bg-paper-muted/70 px-5 py-5">
                <span class="font-mono text-sm text-ink-500">
                  {String(index + 1).padStart(2, '0')}
                </span>
                <h4 class="mt-2 font-serif text-lg font-semibold text-ink-900">{step.title}</h4>
                <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-700">{step.body}</p>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ol>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-4">
        <div class="panel p-6">
          <h3 class="font-serif text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">What is the Supervisor?</h3>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-600">
            The Supervisor is my programmatic solution for an orchestration layer — governable
            AI agent guidance. It does not replace the agents’ reasoning — it governs the
            workflow around them, deciding what must be known, what happens next, and when the
            system should pause, escalate, or continue.
          </p>
        </div>

        <details class="panel group overflow-hidden">
          <summary
            class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 px-6 py-5 [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden"
          >
            <h3 class="font-serif text-xl font-semibold text-ink-900">What is a context bundle?</h3>
            <span
              aria-hidden
              class="grid h-7 w-7 shrink-0 place-items-center rounded-full border border-ink-200 text-ink-600 transition-transform group-open:rotate-45"
              >+</span
            >
          </summary>
          <div class="border-t border-ink-100 px-6 py-5">
            <p class="text-[0.95rem] italic leading-relaxed text-ink-600">
              A context bundle is the package of information an agent receives before it does its
              work. Instead of giving the agent unrestricted access to everything, the system
              assembles a scoped set of instructions, documents, rules, and relevant evidence so
              the agent can reason within the right boundaries.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-12 grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-2">
      <div class="panel p-6">
        <p class="eyebrow mb-4">What the bundle carries</p>
        <ul class="space-y-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-700">
          {executableContext.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="panel p-6">
        <p class="eyebrow mb-4">Why the embedding step stays boring</p>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-600">
          The intelligence of the system is not in the embedding call. It is in the later routing,
          reranking, and bundle assembly where permissions, authority, and source fit are applied
          deliberately.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3 class="mt-16 font-serif text-xl text-ink-900">Operating principles</h3>
    <PrincipleList items={approachPrinciples} />

    <DetailExpander
      summary="This approach does not replace RAG so much as specialize it."
      kind="architecture"
      client:visible
    >
      Enterprise context is too heterogeneous to flatten into one uniform retrieval method, so
      retrieval should be tailored to the source itself. RAG is still vital, especially for
      enterprise data lakes and other large document ecosystems where semantic retrieval is
      indispensable. But when decisions depend on authority, permissions, and source type,
      retrieval should be adapted to the structure of the information itself.
    </DetailExpander>
  </NarrativeSection>

  <NarrativeSection id="scenario" tone="muted" width="wide">
    <SectionHero
      eyebrow={scenario.data.eyebrow}
      title={scenario.data.title}
      lede={scenario.data.lede}
    />
    <div class="prose-narrative mt-8 max-w-3xl">
      <ScenarioBody.Content />
    </div>

    <div class="mt-10 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
      <span class="chip">Path: regulated</span>
      <span class="chip">Outcome: escalated</span>
      <span class="chip">Fast-track: ineligible</span>
      <span class="chip">Approval path: standard</span>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-12 grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2 xl:grid-cols-5">
      {
        scenarioActs.map((act) => (
          <TakeawayCard index={act.index} title={act.title}>
            {act.body}
          </TakeawayCard>
        ))
      }
    </div>

    <Callout kind="rationale" title="Why this scenario works for the presentation">
      It forces the system to demonstrate prerequisite gating, regulated-path classification,
      domain-specific evidence use, escalation preservation, and stakeholder-ready next-step
      guidance. That makes the architecture visible without requiring a live backend.
    </Callout>

    <h3 class="mt-16 font-serif text-xl text-ink-900">Domain agents in the pipeline</h3>
    <div class="mt-6 grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2 xl:grid-cols-5">
      {domainAgents.map((agent) => <TakeawayCard title={agent.title}>{agent.body}</TakeawayCard>)}
    </div>

    <div class="mt-10 flex flex-wrap gap-3 text-sm">
      <a
        href="/demo"
        class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded-full bg-ink-900 px-5 py-3 font-medium text-paper no-underline shadow-soft transition-colors hover:bg-ink-800"
      >
        See the three-pane replay
        <span aria-hidden>→</span>
      </a>
      <a
        href="/findings"
        class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded-full border border-ink-200 bg-paper px-5 py-3 font-medium text-ink-800 no-underline transition-colors hover:border-ink-900"
      >
        Review the findings
      </a>
    </div>
  </NarrativeSection>
</BaseLayout>

